.TH SEED\-TO\-MASTER 1 2026-10-25 "blocktools 0.1.0" "blocktools manual"
.SH NAME
seed\-to\-master \- seed phrase (+ optional passphrase) \-> BIP32 master key(s)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
seed\-to\-master [\-\-passphrase\-prompt] < {"mnemonic":...} lines \-> master keys (BIP39 and Electrum standard/segwit; 2FA and old\-Electrum: unsupported, planned)
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
stdin JSON lines: optional {"passphrase":"..."} then {"mnemonic":"...","candidate":N}, or a bare phrase line.
A phrase is tried as BIP39 AND as an Electrum (2.x+) seed; every valid reading is
one output line with its "scheme". If the phrase has extra words (e.g. a note
line), the trailing 12/13/15/18/21/24 words are tried too (sliding window).
\-\-passphrase\-prompt asks the human on /dev/tty with echo off (never argv/env).
Output CONTAINS SECRETS ("xprv", "seed"). One job: phrase \-> master key.
.SH WARNING
DANGEROUS: use at your own risk. Code that handles keys and signs transactions should always be read and audited by you before you trust it with real funds. Used incorrectly, it can destroy your money.
.br
Used incorrectly (wrong address, huge fee, lost keys, wrong chain) you lose funds; verify every transaction.
.br
It opens hostile wallet files and extracts secrets: run it only sealed and offline (./blocktools run).
.br
Unit tests are evidence, not a substitute for your own review. See docs/SECURITY.md.
.SH SEE ALSO
blocktools(7), docs/SECURITY.md, recipes/README.md
